<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · British Isles</p><h1 class="st-title">Five Boroughs of the Danelaw</h1><p class="st-meta">877 CE – 942 CE (approx.) · capital Derby; Leicester; Lincoln; Nottingham; Stamford</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Five fortified Danish towns of the east Midlands, Derby, Leicester, Lincoln, Nottingham and Stamford, that formed the core of Viking rule south of the Humber.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-heathen-army">Great Heathen Army</a> partitioned <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-mercia">Mercia</a> in 877, Danish warbands settled the east <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/midlands">Midlands</a> around fortified centres. Each borough was the base of a jarl and his army, controlling the surrounding district, in an area later known as part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danelaw">Danelaw</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The boroughs were military and trading centres that grew into towns, with Danish law, land units and many Scandinavian place-names in -by and -thorpe. They acted together at times, but there is no evidence of a single ruler. The settlers soon adopted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aethelflaed">Aethelflaed</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-mercia">Mercia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-the-elder">Edward the Elder</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derby">Derby</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leicester">Leicester</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nottingham">Nottingham</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stamford-lincolnshire">Stamford</a> in 917 and 918. After a Norse revival from York, Edmund of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> recovered the Five Boroughs in 942, an event celebrated in a poem in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxon-chronicle">Anglo-Saxon Chronicle</a>. The region kept distinct legal customs into the 11th century.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/five-boroughs-of-the-danelaw.jpg" alt="Five Boroughs of the Danelaw" width="320" height="271"><figcaption>Image: Robin Boulby. The original uploader was Robinboulby at English Wikipedia. deriva... · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Midland_Map_-_5_Boroughs_912_Ad.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>877 CE – 942 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Derby; Leicester; Lincoln; Nottingham; Stamford</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Norse paganism; Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Norse, Old English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/united-kingdom">United Kingdom</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Five Boroughs</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=910&amp;focus=five-boroughs-of-the-danelaw" aria-label="Five Boroughs of the Danelaw on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4647 355 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Five Boroughs of the Danelaw lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4967 611L4976 582M4967 611L4970 663"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4976 582h0M4970 663h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4967 611h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4967 611h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Derby. <a href="/atlas?year=910&amp;focus=five-boroughs-of-the-danelaw">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

